Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.30,0:00:06.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,$65,320. That's the gap between the\Nluckiest tenth and the unluckiest
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.13,0:00:11.13,Sub,,0,0,0,,of 2,000 versions of the same\Nbusiness, running the same plan for a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.31,0:00:16.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,You'd read the winners' interviews and\Ncall it strategy. It's dice, and I
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.02,0:00:21.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,can show you the dice. Here's the\Nmodel most of us run on. Somebody grew
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.71,0:00:26.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,brand until it was big. They wrote the\Npost, they did the podcast, they
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.63,0:00:31.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,listed the moves. Reorder deeper. Hold\Nthe price. Cut the bottom of the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:31.59,0:00:36.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,catalog. The moves worked for them, so\Nthe moves must be the reason. It's a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.88,0:00:42.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,fair model. It's how we learn nearly\Neverything. Watch someone succeed,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.25,0:00:46.46,Sub,,0,0,0,,do what they did. The problem isn't\Nthe logic. The problem is who we get
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.46,0:00:52.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,watch. So let's find out what the\Nwinners did. Not from an interview.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.70,0:00:58.52,Sub,,0,0,0,,machine that ran the same plan 2,000\Ntimes. One catalog, 24 products, about
Dialogue: 0,0:00:58.52,0:01:05.70,Sub,,0,0,0,,$3.5M a year in sales, $262,000 in the\Nbank on day one. Same prices every
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.70,0:01:11.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,run. Same reorder points. Same ad\Nspend. The only thing that changes
Dialogue: 0,0:01:11.07,0:01:15.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,runs is which customers show up on\Nwhich day. Here's how the year ends.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.62,0:01:21.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,median finished at $969,579. The top\Ntenth finished at $1,014,998. The
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.08,0:01:25.28,Sub,,0,0,0,,bottom tenth, $924,168.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.08,0:01:30.54,Sub,,0,0,0,,Now go and interview the top tenth.\NEvery one of them can tell you,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:30.54,0:01:34.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,truthfully, what they did. They\Nreordered on schedule. They held their
Dialogue: 0,0:01:34.50,0:01:39.56,Sub,,0,0,0,,through the slow months. They kept the\Nads running. And every one of those
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.56,0:01:44.14,Sub,,0,0,0,,things is also what the bottom tenth\Ndid, because it is the same plan. The
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.14,0:01:52.05,Sub,,0,0,0,,gap between $1,014,998 and $924,168 is\Nwhich days the customers came, and
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.05,0:01:57.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,nothing else. That gap is $65,320.\NIt's 7% of the median outcome,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.97,0:02:03.00,Sub,,0,0,0,,by the order the demand arrived in. If\Nyou only ever hear from the top, you
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.00,0:02:07.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,will credit all of it to the moves.\NThe moves were shared. The outcome
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.48,0:02:12.31,Sub,,0,0,0,,wasn't. And the story the winners tell\Nabout the moves will be sincere,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.54,0:02:16.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,detailed, and wrong about what caused\Nwhat.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.35,0:02:20.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,Here's the part that should bother you\Nmore.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.10,0:02:25.57,Sub,,0,0,0,,Look at the worst day of the year for\Nthe winners. The lowest their cash
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.57,0:02:31.38,Sub,,0,0,0,,went was $95,201. Now look at the\Nlowest point for every run, winners
Dialogue: 0,0:02:31.38,0:02:35.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,losers together: $95,201. Same number.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:36.47,0:02:41.10,Sub,,0,0,0,,The top tenth went through the\Nidentical worst day as the bottom
Dialogue: 0,0:02:41.10,0:02:46.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,the same date, for the same reason:\Nthe supplier wires leave before the
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.08,0:02:47.42,Sub,,0,0,0,,platform pays out.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.22,0:02:51.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,Nobody in the winners' circle will\Nmention that day, because they came
Dialogue: 0,0:02:51.78,0:02:56.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,the other side and it stopped\Nmattering. The people it did matter to
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.15,0:03:00.93,Sub,,0,0,0,,giving interviews. That is\Nsurvivorship bias in a sentence. The
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.93,0:03:05.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,can hear from was selected by the\Noutcome you are trying to explain. So
Dialogue: 0,0:03:05.48,0:03:10.67,Sub,,0,0,0,,advice isn't merely incomplete. It is\Ntilted toward whatever risk the
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.67,0:03:16.22,Sub,,0,0,0,,survivors happened to survive. A short\Nlist, and you can do all of it this
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.22,0:03:21.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,week. First, before you copy anyone,\Nask for the denominator. How many
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.94,0:03:27.25,Sub,,0,0,0,,people ran this plan, and how many of\Nthem are not on the podcast. If nobody
Dialogue: 0,0:03:27.25,0:03:32.76,Sub,,0,0,0,,can answer, treat the advice as a\Nstory, not a method. Next, look for
Dialogue: 0,0:03:32.76,0:03:37.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,shared moves. If the winners and the\Nlosers did the same thing, that thing
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.39,0:03:43.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,is not the explanation, however\Nconfidently it's told. Last, run your
Dialogue: 0,0:03:43.40,0:03:47.88,Sub,,0,0,0,,plan more than once. Take your last\Nyear, change nothing but the order the
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.88,0:03:53.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,demand arrived in, and see how wide\Nthe spread gets. If it's wide, most of
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.55,0:03:57.59,Sub,,0,0,0,,what you'd credit to skill is\Nvariance, and the plan should be
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.59,0:04:03.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,worst tenth, not its best. What you\Ncan't do by hand is that last step at
Dialogue: 0,0:04:03.40,0:04:09.45,Sub,,0,0,0,,any real scale. Running a year of a\N24-product catalog 2,000 times, with
Dialogue: 0,0:04:09.45,0:04:14.02,Sub,,0,0,0,,demand that moves together across\Nproducts the way it really does, is a
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.02,0:04:17.85,Sub,,0,0,0,,model, and a bad model is worse than\Nno model.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.65,0:04:23.32,Sub,,0,0,0,,What you can do today is stop asking\Nwinners how they won. Ask how many
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.32,0:04:28.06,Sub,,0,0,0,,people ran their plan, and where the\Nrest of them are. You can build this
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.06,0:04:32.55,Sub,,0,0,0,,yourself. If you're doing real volume\Nand want it run with rigor, this is
Dialogue: 0,0:04:32.55,0:04:34.87,Sub,,0,0,0,,what I do, and I only get paid when it\Nworks.
